  "response": "The human cost establishes the gravity of the review: either no one was killed during the tornado outbreak, or twenty-two people were killed during it; one of those two descriptions is the correct one, and no reconstruction can hold both at once. The National Weather Service conducts formal service assessments after high-impact weather events, and such an assessment gathers the observing-network record, the assimilation diagnostics and the parameterization inventory into a single evidentiary file.\n\nRoutine upper-air radiosonde launches in the United States occur twice daily at 00 and 12 UTC, and that thin fixed cadence is what led to the provision under which forecasters request special off-hour radiosonde launches ahead of anticipated severe weather. Operational radiosondes are launched every three hours at every United States upper-air site; a uniform three-hourly ascent schedule and a fixed pair of launches at 00 and 12 UTC cannot both describe the same network, so one of the two characterizations has to be discarded before the observational record is interpreted at all. Because forecasters are in a position to call for those off-hour ascents, data assimilation blends observations with a background forecast to produce model initial conditions, and that blended analysis is the object any post-event diagnosis must interrogate first.\n\nTwo candidate culprits are then isolated, and at least one of them held during the event, perhaps both: the low-level moisture field in the model analysis was initialized too dry, and the boundary layer scheme mixed the near-surface layer too deeply during the forecast period. Errors in a planetary boundary layer scheme can produce forecast low levels that are too dry, and precisely such an error brought about the excessively dry initialization of the low-level moisture field in the analysis. Observing System Experiments rerun a forecast with selected observations deliberately withheld, and those controlled reruns demonstrate that the missed forecast originated in the model's initial conditions rather than in its physical parameterizations. In other words, the error lay in the analysis supplied to the model and not in the model's representation of physical processes, a verdict that cannot be squared with the boundary layer scheme having mixed the near-surface layer too deeply over the course of the forecast. The resolution of that conflict determines whether the corrective action is an upgrade to the observing and assimilation chain or a revision of the scheme itself, and the service assessment states which of the two it endorses.\n\nAdjudicating between an observational failure and a failure of model physics after an unforecast tornado outbreak requires that the forecast record, the analysis fields and the behaviour of the parameterizations each be reconstructed and tested against the other two. The highest categorical risk level in a Storm Prediction Center convective outlook is \"moderate\", and the distance between the issued categorical designation and the observed severity fixes the magnitude of what has to be explained.",
